  <prompt>One defect class, at least five instances, two of them live. For every path where bytes reach SharePoint Embedded, the SERVER must choose the container from a record it has already authorized the caller against. Find every instance, including the ones not yet listed. Fix them. Prove completeness by absence, not by inventory.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE security engineer. The deliverable is a closed set: after this task, no code path lets a caller name the container its bytes land in, and a mechanical guard makes a new one a build failure.</role>

  <goal>Every SPE write path resolves its container server-side from `(entity, recordId)` via task 075's `RecordContainerResolver`, having authorized the caller on that record first. The two live app-only holes are closed. Compose is converted. A guard prevents regression. Completeness is proven by a grep for absence, not by an inventory that can go stale.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>⭐ **READ `notes/task-076-record-keyed-upload-contract.md` AND `notes/finding-compose-create-on-save-client-named-container.md` FIRST.** They contain the model, the verified facts, and three closed decisions you must not re-litigate.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§1 — THE DEFECT CLASS, AND WHY IT IS ONE CLASS
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

**Shape**: the client names a SharePoint Embedded container; the server writes bytes into it. The
authorization decision (if any) keys on something OTHER than the container, so the two can disagree.

**Why it is permanent damage, not a nuisance**: SPE permissions are **additive-only** — *"you can't break
inheritance on arbitrary files or folders"*. A document written into a shared container is readable by
every member of that container and **no later per-item permission retracts it**. There is no repair.

**Why partial completion is worth little**: one surviving path puts secure content in a shared container
forever. This is why the deliverable is an argument from absence.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§2 — THE KNOWN INVENTORY. Verified 2026-08-27. TWO ROWS ARE UNTRACED.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

| # | Path | Container from | Auth today | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `PUT /api/containers/{containerId}/files/{*path}` (app-only) | client route | none per-resource | ✅ **073 DELETED** |
| 2 | `POST /api/obo/drives/{driveId}/upload-session` + `PUT /api/obo/upload-session/chunk` | client route | `RequireAuthorization()` | ✅ **076 DELETED** (were dead — the drive route they depended on is mapped nowhere) |
| 3 | `PUT /api/obo/containers/{id}/files/{*path}` | client route | `RequireAuthorization()` only | 🔄 **076 converting** — the Create\* wizard path (U1/U2/U3) |
| 4 | **`PUT /api/drives/{driveId}/upload`** (`DocumentsEndpoints.cs:37-95`) | client route | **`canwritefiles` POLICY only** | 🔴 **LIVE. app-only MI.** Waived `UNOWNED`. **DO THIS FIRST** |
| 5 | **`DELETE /api/drives/{driveId}/items/{itemId}`** (`DocumentsEndpoints.cs:~100-148`) | client route | **`canwritefiles` POLICY only** | 🔴 **LIVE. A DESTROY.** Waived `UNOWNED` |
| 6 | Compose create-on-save `SaveComposeDocumentRequest.ContainerId` | client **body** | in-handler | 🔲 issue **#858**. Sequenced behind PR #806 |
| 7 | `ChatWordExportEndpoints.cs:154` | `ResolveDriveIdAsync(containerId, …)` then `UploadSmallAsUserAsync` | OBO | ⚠️ **UNTRACED — trace it** |
| 8 | `ChatDocumentEndpoints.cs:1160` | same shape | OBO | ⚠️ **UNTRACED — trace it** |

**Rows 4 and 5 first, and here is why they outrank Compose.** They write **app-only (MI)**. Task 073's
whole finding was that app-only needs no container ACL — so unlike every OBO row these are **live holes,
not latent bypasses**. Row 5 is a destroy, which is the worst case for a wrong-resource-domain decision.
Both survived 073 purely because they live in `DocumentsEndpoints.cs`, outside its file scope. Neither is
blocked by any open PR (verified: zero overlap).

**Do not trust this table as complete. Finding row 9 is the point of the task.**

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§3 — THE MODEL. Settled by the owner 2026-08-27; do NOT re-open.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

```
secure record      -> its OWN sprk_containerid, or FAIL CLOSED (never a fallback)
everything else    -> the RECORD's owningbusinessunit -> businessunit.sprk_containerid
server-side ingest -> Communication:ArchiveContainerId (no owning record exists)
```

**The container follows the RECORD, not the acting user.** Every client site previously resolved
`getUserId() → systemuser.businessunitid → businessunit.sprk_containerid` — the person uploading, not the
thing uploaded to. Two users uploading to one matter split its documents across two containers. Worse:
users sit in the Operations subtree while secure records are owned in `Secure Projects`, so acting-user
resolution writes secure content into the general **Operations** container.

**Already implemented** by task 076 in `RecordContainerResolver`:
`ResolveForRecordAsync(entity, recordId, ct)` — derives the BU fallback itself. **Reuse it. Do not build a
second resolver** (§11).

**Three things about it that are load-bearing — do not "simplify" any of them:**
1. `SecureContainerDecision.Decide` is PURE and pinned jointly with the TypeScript half against
   `tests/fixtures/secure-container-decision-table.json`. Changing it desyncs both suites.
2. The BU lookup is **skipped for secure records** — so the fail-closed path cannot acquire a usable
   fallback. Not an optimisation.
3. BU read failures **propagate**. "Unknown" must never become "no fallback".

**INV-7 is not a constraint.** `design.md:450` says *"the BFF deliberately does not resolve this
server-side (INV-7)"*. Traced: its only concrete statement is a comment on
`SaveComposeDocumentRequest.ContainerId` reading *"the resolver stays in the wizards"* — a **scope
boundary** from `spaarke-multi-container-multi-index`, cited downstream as a technical limit. **Correcting
`design.md:450` is part of this task.**

**Verified live against Dataverse 2026-08-27 — do not re-derive:** `owningbusinessunit` populated on every
`sprk_project` row · `businessunit.sprk_containerid` populated on 3 of 6 BUs · the `Secure Project` BU has
**no** container (correct — secure records use their own) · the root `Spaarke` BU **shares** its container
with `Spaarke Business Unit 1`, so **a BU container is not an isolation boundary** · `sprk_issecure` is
**NULL on 5 of 10 rows**, the "absent is not false" case, in real data.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§4 — THE AUTHORIZATION PRIMITIVE. Extend, do not add (§11).
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`CallerRecordAccessProbe.GetCallerRightsAsync(callerBearerToken, entitySet, recordId, ct)` → `AccessRights`,
**fail-closed to `None`** when the question cannot be answered. `Api/Filters/EntityAccessFilter.cs` already
wraps it for `(entityType, recordId)`.

Needs a logical-name → entity-SET map. **Reuse `SemanticSearchAuthorizationFilter`'s
`TryResolveParentEntitySet`** — task 080 made it `internal` for exactly this. A second map is a review
failure.

**Rows 4 and 5 are app-only (MI), so there is no caller token to do OBO with.** That is the genuine design
question on those two, and it is why they are `fable` tier rather than mechanical:
- if they have real callers, they need a caller identity to authorize against — determine whether one exists
- if they have **no** callers, DELETE them, as 073 did to their twin and 076 did to the chunked pair
- **Grep for callers before designing anything.** Every count asserted in this project has been wrong at
  least once.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§5 — SEQUENCING. One hard block.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

**PR #806 modifies `IComposeService.cs`, `ComposeEndpoints.cs` AND `ComposeService.cs`** — verified by
unpaginated listing (`gh api --paginate .../pulls/806/files`, 352 files). Row 6 is **sequenced behind
#806**, not delegated. Issue #858 tells compose-r8 to stand down and to notify us when those files are
stable. Rows 4, 5, 7, 8 are unblocked now.

⚠️ **`gh pr view --json files` CAPS AT 100 and reports no error.** It under-reported #806 (352) and #843
(178). Always use `gh api --paginate` for overlap checks in this repo.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§6 — COMPLETENESS: prove it by ABSENCE and by a GUARD
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

An inventory goes stale the day someone adds a route. Two mechanisms instead:

1. **A grep for absence** — no route binds a container/drive id from client input on a write path; no client
   supplies one. This is the acceptance evidence.
2. **A mechanical guard** — extend `tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.cs` with a rule
   that FAILS when a write route binds a container/drive parameter without a per-record decision. Task 074
   proved this works: its census forced the classification that found 077, 078 and 081, and its
   registration pin caught 076's own route deletion before review did.

Requires a **negative control** (seeded violation → rule fires) and a **positive control** (sanctioned
shape → rule does not fire) per `tests/CLAUDE.md`. Task 060's positive controls caught two real defects.</background>
