  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Confirm 076 landed (route 3 converted) before starting, so you are not reshaping a surface mid-change.</step>
    <step order="1">TRACE rows 7 and 8 — where does `containerId` come from in `ChatWordExportEndpoints.cs:154` and `ChatDocumentEndpoints.cs:1160`? Classify each: client-supplied (fix), server-derived (fine), or dead (delete). These are the two the previous session explicitly did NOT trace.</step>
    <step order="2">SWEEP for unlisted instances. Do not grep for `containerId` alone — that finds reads too. Classify by SINK: find every call to a write/create/delete operation on SPE, then walk backwards to where its container or drive id came from. `UploadSmall*`, `CreateUploadSession*`, `ReplaceFileContent*`, `DeleteItem*`, and any Graph drive-item mutation.</step>
    <step order="3">Rows 4 and 5 FIRST — grep for callers. No callers → delete (like 073 and 076 did). Callers exist → determine what caller identity is available on an app-only route, and design the decision. This is the escalation-worthy judgment call.</step>
    <step order="4">Fix each remaining row: authorize `(entity, recordId)` via a filter, then resolve the container via `RecordContainerResolver.ResolveForRecordAsync(entity, recordId, ct)`.</step>
    <step order="5">Row 6 (Compose) when #806 clears — thread `(entity, recordId)` onto `SaveComposeDocumentRequest` from the session that already holds `MatterId` (ADR-040 binding), resolve server-side, delete `ContainerId` and the client call feeding it. Close #858.</step>
    <step order="6">Add the ArchTest guard (§6) with negative AND positive controls.</step>
    <step order="7">Correct `design.md:450`'s INV-7 claim and any doc repeating it.</step>
    <step order="8">Tests per row: caller without record access DENIED · secure record → own container · secure record with null container FAILS rather than falling back · non-secure unchanged. Perturbation-check the deny case: break the gate, confirm a test reddens.</step>
    <step order="9">The absence-grep (§6.1). Build, full suite, publish size, CVE. Record the final inventory with every row's disposition.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If a write path genuinely has NO owning record at the moment the bytes move — so `(entity, recordId)` cannot be supplied — STOP. That is the modelling gap this class exists to close; if one survives, the contract needs a different shape (a server-issued upload ticket, say) and that is an owner decision. Do NOT reintroduce a client-supplied container "just for that path" — that is option (B) through the back door.</trigger>
    <trigger>If rows 4/5 turn out to have live callers AND no caller identity is available on the app-only route, STOP. Authorizing an app-only write against a record requires an identity; inventing one is worse than the hole.</trigger>
    <trigger>If the sweep finds more than ~3 unlisted instances, STOP and re-plan before fixing. That would mean the class is broader than a task and needs its own phase.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>075 provides the resolver (now deriving the BU fallback itself, per 076). `CallerRecordAccessProbe` + `EntityAccessFilter` authorize `(entityType, recordId)`. `RouteAuthorizationGuardTests` provides the guard harness. All five-plus write paths exist.</existing>
    <extension>Route contract changes and deletions on existing surface, plus ONE new ArchTest rule extending an existing guard class. No new service, no new endpoint, no new package.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Named, live, and app-only: `PUT /api/drives/{driveId}/upload` writes bytes into a caller-named container as the MI, needing no container ACL, gated only by a policy whose resource domain does not match the route key. `DELETE /api/drives/{driveId}/items/{itemId}` destroys on the same terms. Because SPE permissions are additive-only, the first secure project whose content lands in a shared container is unrecoverable — and the window closes on FIRST USE, not on a date.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <outputs>
    <output type="doc">The complete classified inventory — every SPE write path, its container source, its disposition. Including rows found by the sweep.</output>
    <output type="code">Rows 4, 5, 7, 8 fixed or deleted; row 6 fixed when #806 clears</output>
    <output type="code">ArchTest rule + negative and positive controls</output>
    <output type="code">`design.md:450` INV-7 correction</output>
    <output type="test">Per row: deny-without-access, secure→own, secure+null→fail-closed, non-secure unchanged</output>
    <output type="doc">The absence-grep evidence</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>No write path binds a container or drive id from client input. Proven by grep for absence, not by inventory.</criterion>
    <criterion>Every remaining write path authorizes the caller on the owning record BEFORE resolving a container, and resolves via 075's resolver.</criterion>
    <criterion>Rows 4 and 5 are closed — gated or deleted — and their `UNOWNED` waivers are gone, not converted to Permanent.</criterion>
    <criterion>Rows 7 and 8 are traced and classified. "Not traced" is not an acceptable end state.</criterion>
    <criterion>Row 6 is fixed and #858 closed, OR #858 carries a dated note explaining what #806 is still blocking.</criterion>
    <criterion>The ArchTest guard fails on a seeded violation (negative control) and passes on the sanctioned shape (positive control).</criterion>
    <criterion>`design.md:450` no longer states INV-7 as a technical constraint.</criterion>
    <criterion>No finding is handed to another project as its fix. Notifications are fine; delegating the work is not.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>The forcing function has now produced four holes (077, 078, 081, and this class) that no human sweep found. Trust it over any inventory, including §2's. The reason completeness is the deliverable rather than the fixes themselves: SPE cannot retract, so one survivor is permanent — and the argument from absence is the only one a future contributor cannot silently regress.</notes>
</task>
